/-!
# Artin's induction theorem

For a finite group `G`, the sharp Artin identity and the projection formula show that
`|G| • χ` is induced from virtual characters of cyclic subgroups for every virtual character `χ`.
The target is the canonical subgroup
`TauCeti.ClassFunction.indVirtualCharacters k G (fun C ↦ IsCyclic C)`, rather than a parallel
cyclic-specific construction.

The direct sum of induction maps `⨁_{C cyclic} R(C) → R(G)` is also bundled explicitly.  After
tensoring source and target over `ℤ` with `ℚ`, its scalar extension is surjective.  Thus the
usual rational formulation is available as a map-level theorem without taking `k = ℚ`.

For comparison with the earlier spanning development, this file retains the finer subgroup
generated by characters induced from irreducible characters of cyclic subgroups.  Over an
algebraically closed characteristic-zero field it agrees with the canonical target.  The original
lattice-descent proof giving an unspecified common denominator remains as an
implementation-strength refinement.

The sharp identity is proved by Möbius inversion on the finite poset of cyclic subgroups, and the
projection formula propagates it from the trivial character to every virtual character.  The
separate generator-span refinement turns the `k`-linear spanning statement of
`TauCeti.RepresentationTheory.Induction.Spanning` into an arithmetic one by a lattice descent.  The
characters induced from the cyclic subgroups span the class functions over `k`, so a subfamily of
them is a `k`-basis; that subfamily has as many members as the basis of irreducible characters
`TauCeti.basisIrreducibleCharacter`, and, being characters, its members lie in the `ℤ`-lattice that
the irreducible characters span.  The integer matrix expressing them in that basis is invertible
over `k`, so its determinant `d` is a nonzero integer, and multiplying by the adjugate expresses
`d • χᵢ` over the induced characters with integer coefficients.

## Main statements

* `TauCeti.ClassFunction.natCard_nsmul_mem_indVirtualCharacters_isCyclic`: **Artin's induction
  theorem, sharp form**, through the canonical induced-virtual-character API.
* `TauCeti.ClassFunction.indVirtualCharactersAddHom`: the bundled direct-sum induction map for any
  family of subgroups.
* `TauCeti.ClassFunction.cyclicInductionRatLinearMap_surjective`: rational surjectivity after
  extension of scalars from `ℤ` to `ℚ`.
* `TauCeti.ClassFunction.indVirtualCharacters_isCyclic_eq_indVirtualCharacterSpanCyclic`: over an
  algebraically closed field in which `|G|` is invertible, the cyclic-induced virtual characters
  are exactly the subgroup generated by induced irreducible characters.
* `TauCeti.ClassFunction.exists_sum_zsmul_ind_ofCharacter_eq_zsmul_cyclic`: the fixed finite-family
  lattice implementation refinement.
* `TauCeti.ClassFunction.natCard_nsmul_mem_indVirtualCharacters_isElementary`: the sharp theorem,
  read for the elementary subgroups.

## Implementation notes

The scalar field `k` and group `G` may lie in different universes.  The FDRep induction plumbing
uses a small model of Mathlib's induced representation and exposes an equivariant comparison with
the original possibly universe-large carrier.

The lattice descent is a general statement about a `ℤ`-lattice inside a finite-dimensional vector
space and has nothing to do with characters, but it is kept `private` here rather than given a home
of its own in the linear-algebra hierarchy, there being exactly one use of it.

## References

This proves both parts of the "Artin's induction theorem" item of Layer 6 in
`TauCetiRoadmap/RepresentationTheory/InductionRestriction/README.md`: rational surjectivity of the
direct-sum induction map and `ℤ`-membership of `|G| • χ` in the cyclic-induced subgroup.

* J.-P. Serre, *Linear Representations of Finite Groups*, Springer GTM 42 (1977), Section 9.2,
  Theorem 17 and its corollary.
* C. W. Curtis, I. Reiner, *Methods of Representation Theory, Vol. I*, Wiley (1981), Section 15.
-/

